WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
Is it possible to make a&nbsp;
Billboard #1 song in 30 seconds?

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.960
Recently people have started&nbsp;
speedrunning hit songs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.020
where you compete to see who can&nbsp;
recreate the beat the fastest.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.220
One of the most popular challenges&nbsp;
is Soulja Boy’s Crank That from 2007.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.540
First people started bragging about&nbsp;
recreating the beat in 2 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.980
then 1 minute, then… 33.76 seconds.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.160
The practice of speedrunning&nbsp;
comes from video games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.520
and it’s not a coincidence that people&nbsp;
have started speedrunning Crank That.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.400
“Crank That” was created&nbsp;
on the software FL Studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.660
and the developer behind FL Studio&nbsp;
was actually a video game developer.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.940
Didier Dambrin is the award-winning game&nbsp;
developer that created FL studio in 1997.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.820
FL Studio is known for being really easy to use,&nbsp;
Dambrin himself calls it “an app for cheaters.”

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.860
So these days, just like speedrunning a video&nbsp;
game, producers can make beats faster than ever.

